<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!--
    Merged into the generated iOS Info.plist by Tauri, which picks up both
    Info.plist and Info.ios.plist from beside tauri.conf.json.

    iOS-only, and that is the point of the separate file: on macOS the bundle
    already declares this scheme from `plugins.deep-link.desktop.schemes`, so
    putting it in the shared Info.plist would declare it twice there.

    Nothing else claims the OAuth callback scheme on iOS. The config key is
    `desktop.schemes` and means it; the plugin's `mobile` key is for universal
    links, which are https and need a domain serving an apple-app-site-
    association file. And `claim_deep_link_schemes` in lib.rs — which does
    register the scheme at runtime for desktop development — calls
    `register_all`, which is desktop-only and answers `UnsupportedPlatform`
    here, exactly as it does on macOS.

    So without this key the authorization server redirects to
    `org.matrixaxon.axon:/oauth/callback`, iOS finds no handler, and the sign-in
    ends in Safari on a page that will not open. A release build fails the same
    way: this is not the development loop missing something an installer would
    have done.

    The scheme is the one in `src/platform/tauri.ts`'s `OAUTH_CLIENT` and in
    `plugins.deep-link.desktop.schemes`. All three have to agree, and the
    operator's server needs the matching `redirect_uris` entry.
  -->
  <key>CFBundleURLTypes</key>
  <array>
    <dict>
      <key>CFBundleURLName</key>
      <string>org.matrixaxon.axon</string>
      <key>CFBundleTypeRole</key>
      <string>Editor</string>
      <key>CFBundleURLSchemes</key>
      <array>
        <string>org.matrixaxon.axon</string>
      </array>
    </dict>
  </array>
</dict>
</plist>
